================================================================================
                    A DUSTY WORKSPACE
================================================================================

You enter a cluttered workspace filled with half-finished
projects and dusty tools.  A workbench sits in the center,
covered with plans for building and organizing.

This is a place of creation and destruction — where you
learn to shape the dungeon itself.

Dust motes float in the dim light.  On the far wall,
ancient diagrams show the art of constructing rooms
and forging objects from nothing.

A sign above the workbench reads:

    "To master the terminal, you must learn
     to CREATE before you DESTROY."

================================================================================
                    CREATION SPELLS
================================================================================

COMMAND            PURPOSE                      DESCRIPTION
--------           --------                     -----------
mkdir NAME         Create a room                Make a new directory
touch NAME         Create an object             Make a new empty file
cp SRC DEST        Duplicate an object          Copy a file
echo "text" > file Write words into a file      Redirect text to a file

================================================================================
                    DESTRUCTION SPELLS
================================================================================

COMMAND            PURPOSE                      DESCRIPTION
--------           --------                     -----------
rm NAME            Destroy an object            Delete a file
rmdir NAME         Collapse a room              Delete an empty directory
rm -r NAME         Obliterate a room            Delete directory and contents

       *** WARNING: rm CANNOT BE UNDONE! ***
       *** There is no "undo" in the terminal! ***

Always use 'ls' to check what is in a room BEFORE deleting.
In the real world, accidentally removing the wrong file
can cause serious problems.

================================================================================
                    WORKSHOP CHALLENGES
================================================================================

Complete these five challenges in order.  Verify your
work at each step by running: ls -F

CHALLENGE 1: Build a storage room
    mkdir storage

CHALLENGE 2: Create a note inside it
    touch storage/note.txt

CHALLENGE 3: Write to the note (using echo and redirection)
    echo "My first note" > storage/note.txt

CHALLENGE 4: Read your note
    cat storage/note.txt

CHALLENGE 5: Clean up when done
    rm storage/note.txt
    rmdir storage

================================================================================
                    ADVANCED: REDIRECTION
================================================================================

The > symbol redirects output INTO a file:

    echo "hello" > greeting.txt    Write "hello" to a file
    cat scroll > backup.txt        Copy scroll contents to backup

The >> symbol APPENDS to a file (does not overwrite):

    echo "line 1" > log.txt       Create file with first line
    echo "line 2" >> log.txt      Add second line

You can view the result:
    cat log.txt

TRY THIS EXERCISE:

    echo "I was here" > journal.txt
    echo "The workshop taught me mkdir" >> journal.txt
    echo "and also touch and rm" >> journal.txt
    cat journal.txt

When you are done experimenting, clean up:
    rm journal.txt

================================================================================
                    VIEWING LONG FILES
================================================================================

You may have noticed that 'cat scroll' causes this long
text to rush past your eyes.  For long files, use:

COMMAND            PURPOSE
--------           --------
less scroll        Page through text slowly
                   SPACE = next page
                   b     = previous page
                   q     = quit

head scroll        Show only the first 10 lines
tail scroll        Show only the last 10 lines
wc scroll          Count lines, words, and characters

These are essential tools for reading long files,
configuration files, and log files in the real world.

